{
 "case": "BKP-COVERAGE",
 "says": "Alice's laptop takes backups under a configured recovery objective of one hour. She registers her private notes source and commits a revision on the head the registration made, a backup is taken ten minutes later, and in the half hour after it she commits a second revision and keeps a hand-written draft — private work that exists only on the laptop. The laptop's live database is then lost. On a rebuilt runtime the backup is restored, and the report states exactly the frontier it covers — the receipt sequence and the instant of the backup it restored, the same values the backup set names — together with the recovery objective, work after the backup as not known, and authority passive. A query for the first commit returns its original result and receipt. Queries for the second commit's and the draft's request ids each return request_not_covered, naming the restore report whose frontier bounds what the owner holds — not request_not_held, which would say they were never received. A commit on the restored head, which would build over the second revision made after the backup, is not accepted: the restored copy is passive, locked at a generation past the restored one, and answers access_locked and access_generation_moved. What must not happen: the report or any answer treating the second revision or the draft as absent, recovered or safe to discard — nothing here moves the head past the covered frontier or drops that work.",
